records[456]:
  - id: "project:project-postcraft:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's PostCraft project?
    text: "Montasim built PostCraft, a website project. AI-powered social media manager to generate, preview, score, and auto-publish engaging posts for LinkedIn, X, and Facebook using Gemini AI and custom brand guardrails. His role covered product design and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning AI workflow, publishing integrations, and background jobs. One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:problem","case-study:postcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "AI-powered social media manager to generate, preview, score, and auto-publish engaging posts for LinkedIn, X, and Facebook using Gemini AI and custom brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq API\nZhipuAI\nInngest\nREST APIs\nTailwind","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status.","One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery.\nProvider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint.\nPublishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation.\nOperational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access."
  - id: "project:project-postcraft:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for PostCraft?
    text: "Montasim built PostCraft with Next.js, React, TypeScript, MongoDB, Mongoose, Redux Toolkit, Better Auth, OpenRouter, Gemini API, Groq API, ZhipuAI, Inngest, REST APIs, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, Zod, Resend, System Design. The product goal is clear: AI-powered social media manager to generate, preview, score, and auto-publish engaging posts for LinkedIn, X, and Facebook using Gemini AI and custom brand guardrails. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: The Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules, infrastructure in core services, and durable delivery in Inngest. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "AI-powered social media manager to generate, preview, score, and auto-publish engaging posts for LinkedIn, X, and Facebook using Gemini AI and custom brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq API\nZhipuAI\nInngest\nREST APIs\nTailwind","Generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews\nWorkspace\nValidated workflows for content, library, accounts, and publishing\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI with model and key fallback\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persistence, Inngest jobs, and social platform APIs\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules, infrastructure in core services, and durable delivery in Inngest.\nA provider switcher walks configured models and"
  - id: "project:project-b4joinacompany:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's b4joinacompany project?
    text: "Montasim built b4joinacompany, a website project. Research a company before you apply, interview, or accept an offer. His role covered product design, evidence modeling, and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning decision support, cited AI, private research, and versioned data. Visitors can research a company without creating an account and open original evidence for important claims. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-b4joinacompany","case-study:b4joinacompany:problem","case-study:b4joinacompany:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Research a company before you apply, interview, or accept an offer.\n\ntrue\n2026-07-26T04:31:13Z\n9849284bf1ac0b399c7b9d9f59bbbb9b36ba9629\n2026-07-26T04:30:07Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/b4joinacompany\nproject-b4joinacompany\nassets/projects/b4joinacompany.png\nhttps://b4joinacompany.netlify.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nMongoDB\nGemini API\nGroq API\nBetter Auth\nREST APIs\nZod\nVitest\nSystem Design\nb4joinacompany\ncareer-tools\ncompany-research\ndue-diligence\nemployment\njob-search\nweb-application\nwebsite","Job seekers in Bangladesh often assemble company research from workplace stories, salary submissions, official pages, and interview conversations. Those sources have different levels of reliability, but conventional summaries can flatten them into an unfair good-or-bad judgment.\nPersonal workplace reports provide context, not verified statements of current company policy.\nCompany names and salary records can be ambiguous, so fuzzy matching cannot silently publish a result.\nAI answers must remain bounded by","Visitors can research a company without creating an account and open original evidence for important claims.\nPrepared questions are generated by fixed rules, while Ask provides cited synthesis or an explicit evidence gap.\nSaved checkpoints retain the exact evidence version and append revisions on successful updates.\nThe product supports comparison without producing a synthetic company score or declaring a winner."
  - id: "project:project-b4joinacompany:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for b4joinacompany?
    text: "Montasim built b4joinacompany with Next.js, React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, MongoDB, Gemini API, Groq API, Better Auth, REST APIs, Zod, Vitest, System Design. The product goal is clear: Research a company before you apply, interview, or accept an offer. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Versioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while private checkpoints live separately in MongoDB. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-b4joinacompany","case-study:b4joinacompany:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Research a company before you apply, interview, or accept an offer.\n\ntrue\n2026-07-26T04:31:13Z\n9849284bf1ac0b399c7b9d9f59bbbb9b36ba9629\n2026-07-26T04:30:07Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/b4joinacompany\nproject-b4joinacompany\nassets/projects/b4joinacompany.png\nhttps://b4joinacompany.netlify.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nMongoDB\nGemini API\nGroq API\nBetter Auth\nREST APIs\nZod\nVitest\nSystem Design\nb4joinacompany\ncareer-tools\ncompany-research\ndue-diligence\nemployment\njob-search\nweb-application\nwebsite","Versioned company, story, comment, and salary evidence records\nSource releases\nDeterministic identity matching, evidence labels, and question ranking\nResearch layer\nNext.js research briefs, compare, Ask, and a versioned REST API\nProduct surfaces\nBetter Auth and MongoDB checkpoints pinned to evidence revisions\nPrivate workspace\nVersioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while private checkpoints live separately in MongoDB.\nReported,"
  - id: "project:project-devtools:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's DevTools project?
    text: "Montasim built DevTools, a tool project. A comprehensive suite of 30+ free developer tools for formatting, validating, generating, and transforming data. All tools run entirely in your browser. His role covered product design and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning developer utilities, shared interaction patterns, and account-backed tools. More than 50 workflows are available through one catalog and command-search model. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-devtools","case-study:devtools:problem","case-study:devtools:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A comprehensive suite of 30+ free developer tools for formatting, validating, generating, and transforming data. All tools run entirely in your browser.\n\ntrue\n2026-04-08T05:59:03Z\n88e28b53c9c618f016edbaada4918442f070d25e\n2026-04-12T09:19:56Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/devtools\nproject-devtools\nassets/projects/devtools.png\nhttps://devtoolsn.vercel.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nCodeMirror 6\nTanStack Query\nPostgreSQL\nPrisma\nBetter Auth\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nZod\nVitest\nGitHub Actions\nCI/CD\nSystem","Small development tasks often send people across unrelated single-purpose websites. Each tool has a different interface, unclear privacy behavior, and no shared path for search, history, saved work, or reuse.\nMany transformations should stay in the browser, while network diagnostics necessarily contact a target or server endpoint.\nA catalog with more than 50 workflows must remain quick to search, navigate, and extend without duplicating page infrastructure.\nSaved items, shares, webhooks, and short links introduce","More than 50 workflows are available through one catalog and command-search model.\nCommon transformations can run without an account and without sending their current input to the server.\nServer-backed features add reuse and sharing without obscuring when data leaves the browser.\nThe architecture supports both small local utilities and stateful workflows without forcing them through one execution path."
  - id: "project:project-devtools:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for DevTools?
    text: "Montasim built DevTools with Next.js, React, TypeScript, CodeMirror 6, TanStack Query, PostgreSQL, Prisma, Better Auth, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, Zod, Vitest, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, System Design. The product goal is clear: A comprehensive suite of 30+ free developer tools for formatting, validating, generating, and transforming data. All tools run entirely in your browser. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A navigation registry acts as the catalog source of truth. Tool features own their tabs and logic, shared app infrastructure handles auth and storage, and route handlers isolate network and persistence work. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-devtools","case-study:devtools:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A comprehensive suite of 30+ free developer tools for formatting, validating, generating, and transforming data. All tools run entirely in your browser.\n\ntrue\n2026-04-08T05:59:03Z\n88e28b53c9c618f016edbaada4918442f070d25e\n2026-04-12T09:19:56Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/devtools\nproject-devtools\nassets/projects/devtools.png\nhttps://devtoolsn.vercel.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nCodeMirror 6\nTanStack Query\nPostgreSQL\nPrisma\nBetter Auth\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nZod\nVitest\nGitHub Actions\nCI/CD\nSystem","Searchable navigation, routes, labels, and keyboard discovery\nTool registry\nFocused formatters, generators, testers, inspectors, and references\nFeature modules\nLocal transforms, preferences, input history, and reusable state\nBrowser boundary\nNext.js APIs, Better Auth, PostgreSQL, Prisma, and target services\nServer boundary\nA navigation registry acts as the catalog source of truth. Tool features own their tabs and logic, shared app infrastructure handles auth and storage, and route handlers isolate network and"
  - id: "project:project-skillfoliox:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog project?
    text: "Montasim built Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog, a skill project. A curated portfolio of reusable SKILL.md capabilities for Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Cursor, and other AI coding agents. His role covered agent workflow design and automation engineering, with scope spanning agent workflow, validation automation, and packaging. Visitors can browse and compare skills. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-skillfoliox","case-study:skillfoliox:problem","case-study:skillfoliox:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A curated portfolio of reusable SKILL.md capabilities for Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Cursor, and other AI coding agents.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-07T09:40:04Z\n37d1ca4ca2816c4e8b08d2e24402c6a2f0e0469f\n2026-08-07T09:47:38Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Skillfoliox\nproject-skillfoliox\nassets/projects/skillfoliox.png\nhttps://skillfoliox.netlify.app\n\nTanStack Start\nReact\nTypeScript\nVite\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nReact Markdown\nVitest\nNetlify\nReact Testing Library\nSystem Design\nSkillfoliox - Agent Skill","Reusable SKILL.md capabilities are scattered across repositories and are difficult to compare by purpose, host compatibility, maturity, and install path.\nCatalog facts can drift from source repositories and package releases.\nRemote GitHub README fetching can fail or be rate-limited.\nHeterogeneous Markdown must render safely and consistently without implying every skill has identical support.","Visitors can browse and compare skills.\nOpen rendered documentation and canonical repositories.\nInspect compatibility, version, status, and categories.\nMaintainers can add catalog entries through a documented, testable workflow."
  - id: "project:project-skillfoliox:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog?
    text: "Montasim built Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog with TanStack Start, React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, React Markdown, Vitest, Netlify, React Testing Library, System Design. The product goal is clear: A curated portfolio of reusable SKILL.md capabilities for Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Cursor, and other AI coding agents. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: TanStack Start routes and React catalog UI; typed local catalog/search/filter model; remote README fetch/render pipeline with retry/source fallback; build-time metadata, sitemap, and Netlify deployment. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-skillfoliox","case-study:skillfoliox:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A curated portfolio of reusable SKILL.md capabilities for Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Cursor, and other AI coding agents.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-07T09:40:04Z\n37d1ca4ca2816c4e8b08d2e24402c6a2f0e0469f\n2026-08-07T09:47:38Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Skillfoliox\nproject-skillfoliox\nassets/projects/skillfoliox.png\nhttps://skillfoliox.netlify.app\n\nTanStack Start\nReact\nTypeScript\nVite\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nReact Markdown\nVitest\nNetlify\nReact Testing Library\nSystem Design\nSkillfoliox - Agent Skill","TanStack Start routes and React catalog UI.\nSkill workflow\nTyped local catalog/search/filter model.\nAutomation\nRemote README fetch/render pipeline with retry/source fallback.\nVerification\nBuild-time metadata, sitemap, and Netlify deployment.\nDistribution\nTanStack Start routes and React catalog UI; typed local catalog/search/filter model; remote README fetch/render pipeline with retry/source fallback; build-time metadata, sitemap, and Netlify deployment.\nKeep discovery metadata in a curated local catalog for"
  - id: "project:project-bangladesh-location-registry:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Bangladesh Location Registry project?
    text: "Montasim built Bangladesh Location Registry, a dataset project. Versioned, provenance-rich Bangladesh location registry with census, postal, facility, ward, and infrastructure data from government-published sources. His role covered data modeling, validation, and release engineering, with scope spanning provenance, transformation, validation, and distribution. Consumers can download structured location data. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-bangladesh-location-registry","case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:problem","case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Versioned, provenance-rich Bangladesh location registry with census, postal, facility, ward, and infrastructure data from government-published sources.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-04T09:46:31Z\n06438720f285a7904f4778af3d30ae827cfdcc8d\n2026-08-04T09:46:58Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/bangladesh-location-registry\nproject-bangladesh-location-registry\nassets/projects/bangladesh-location-registry.png\nhttps://address-bd.netlify.app\n\nJSON\nJSONL\nSQLite\nJavaScript\nNode.js\nGitHub Actions\nCI/CD\nBangladesh Location","Bangladesh location records are spread across differently structured sources, making normalized lookup, provenance review, and repeatable machine consumption difficult.\nAdministrative boundaries, names, and facilities change.\nSource coverage and confidence vary and do not establish authoritative identity/address truth.\nHealth, emergency, eligibility, routing, or legal use requires current authoritative confirmation.","Consumers can download structured location data.\nValidate records against schemas.\nChoose JSON, streaming JSONL, or SQLite.\nTrace coverage and provenance limitations before use."
  - id: "project:project-bangladesh-location-registry:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Bangladesh Location Registry?
    text: "Montasim built Bangladesh Location Registry with JSON, JSONL, SQLite, JavaScript, Node.js, GitHub Actions, CI/CD. The product goal is clear: Versioned, provenance-rich Bangladesh location registry with census, postal, facility, ward, and infrastructure data from government-published sources. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Source/provenance manifests; normalized records governed by JSON Schema; JavaScript validation/build scripts; versioned JSON, JSONL, compressed, and SQLite release outputs with samples/docs. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-bangladesh-location-registry","case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Versioned, provenance-rich Bangladesh location registry with census, postal, facility, ward, and infrastructure data from government-published sources.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-04T09:46:31Z\n06438720f285a7904f4778af3d30ae827cfdcc8d\n2026-08-04T09:46:58Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/bangladesh-location-registry\nproject-bangladesh-location-registry\nassets/projects/bangladesh-location-registry.png\nhttps://address-bd.netlify.app\n\nJSON\nJSONL\nSQLite\nJavaScript\nNode.js\nGitHub Actions\nCI/CD\nBangladesh Location","Source/provenance manifests.\nSource records\nNormalized records governed by JSON Schema.\nTransformation\nJavaScript validation/build scripts.\nRelease formats\nVersioned JSON, JSONL, compressed, and SQLite release outputs with samples/docs.\nValidation\nSource/provenance manifests; normalized records governed by JSON Schema; JavaScript validation/build scripts; versioned JSON, JSONL, compressed, and SQLite release outputs with samples/docs.\nPreserve source lineage and limitations beside normalized data instead of hiding"
  - id: "project:project-ship-agent-skill:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's ship-agent-skill project?
    text: "Montasim built ship-agent-skill, a skill project. End-to-end Codex and Claude Code skill for validating, packaging, publishing, releasing, and verifying portable Agent Skills. His role covered agent workflow design and automation engineering, with scope spanning agent workflow, validation automation, and packaging. Agents can prepare without publishing. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-ship-agent-skill","case-study:ship-agent-skill:problem","case-study:ship-agent-skill:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "End-to-end Codex and Claude Code skill for validating, packaging, publishing, releasing, and verifying portable Agent Skills.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-07T18:35:00Z\n579c49367e6ab254b48c22cc9be74815eb58437e\n2026-08-07T18:35:16Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/ship-agent-skill\nproject-ship-agent-skill\nhttps://www.npmjs.com/package/ship-agent-skill\nhttps://www.npmjs.com/package/ship-agent-skill\nAgent Skills\nJavaScript\nNode.js\nnpm\nGitHub","Releasing a skill spans mutable local state and several independent remote systems, making partial success, version drift, and accidental publication easy.\nPublishing, pushing, releases, deployment, and catalog changes need separate authorization.\nCodex and Claude Code require different install destinations but one portable payload.\nAn immutable artifact may already exist when a later stage fails.","Agents can prepare without publishing.\nExecute only authorized stages.\nDistinguish partial remote success.\nVerify tag, GitHub release, npm package, install command, and catalog state from the consumer side."
  - id: "project:project-ship-agent-skill:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for ship-agent-skill?
    text: "Montasim built ship-agent-skill with Agent Skills, JavaScript, Node.js, npm, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, Git. The product goal is clear: End-to-end Codex and Claude Code skill for validating, packaging, publishing, releasing, and verifying portable Agent Skills. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Agent-facing orchestration in SKILL.md; deterministic preflight/release-gate references; npm installer/package contract and consumer-side tests. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-ship-agent-skill","case-study:ship-agent-skill:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "End-to-end Codex and Claude Code skill for validating, packaging, publishing, releasing, and verifying portable Agent Skills.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-07T18:35:00Z\n579c49367e6ab254b48c22cc9be74815eb58437e\n2026-08-07T18:35:16Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/ship-agent-skill\nproject-ship-agent-skill\nhttps://www.npmjs.com/package/ship-agent-skill\nhttps://www.npmjs.com/package/ship-agent-skill\nAgent Skills\nJavaScript\nNode.js\nnpm\nGitHub","Agent-facing orchestration in SKILL.md.\nSkill workflow\nDeterministic preflight/release-gate references.\nAutomation\nNpm installer/package contract and consumer-side tests.\nVerification\nAgent-facing orchestration in SKILL.md; deterministic preflight/release-gate references; npm installer/package contract and consumer-side tests.\nModel release stages separately rather than treating \"ship\" as blanket permission.\nModel release stages separately\nValidate host portability before creating immutable artifacts.\nValidate"
  - id: "project:project-vidquery:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's VidQuery - YouTube Q&A Extension project?
    text: "Montasim built VidQuery - YouTube Q&A Extension, an extension project. Chromium extension that adds Gemini-powered Q&A to YouTube using visible video metadata, transcripts, and the user's own API key. His role covered product design and browser-extension engineering, with scope spanning browser integration, provider workflows, and privacy. Users can ask questions about an open YouTube video. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-vidquery","case-study:vidquery:problem","case-study:vidquery:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Chromium extension that adds Gemini-powered Q&A to YouTube using visible video metadata, transcripts, and the user's own API key.\n\nfalse\n2025-08-26T19:20:17Z\n56548b55d75dfd868310994f001c2650f541ee3f\n2025-08-26T19:20:14Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/VidQuery\nproject-vidquery\nassets/projects/vidquery.png\nhttps://vidquery.netlify.app\n\nhttps://github.com/montasim/VidQuery/releases/latest\nWXT\nTypeScript\nReact 19\nChrome Manifest V3\nChrome Extensions API\nTailwind CSS\nGemini API\nZod\nTanStack Start\nVitest\nReact Testing","Viewers must scrub long YouTube videos to find specific answers even when the page already exposes metadata and a transcript.\nAnswers are limited by transcript availability and quality.\nGemini calls require the user's own API key and send bounded video context to an external provider.\nYouTube route/DOM changes and extension permission boundaries can break extraction.","Users can ask questions about an open YouTube video.\nAnswers can use available transcript and visible metadata.\nUsers control their own provider credential.\nMaintainers can produce installable GitHub release artifacts."
  - id: "project:project-vidquery:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for VidQuery - YouTube Q&A Extension?
    text: "Montasim built VidQuery - YouTube Q&A Extension with WXT, TypeScript, React 19, Chrome Manifest V3, Chrome Extensions API, Tailwind CSS, Gemini API, Zod, TanStack Start, Vitest, React Testing Library, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, Git, System Design. The product goal is clear: Chromium extension that adds Gemini-powered Q&A to YouTube using visible video metadata, transcripts, and the user's own API key. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: YouTube content extraction; React extension UI and request state; validated transcript/context envelope and Gemini adapter; browser storage/permissions plus release-built Manifest V3 package. The companion web app documents and distributes the product. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-vidquery","case-study:vidquery:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Chromium extension that adds Gemini-powered Q&A to YouTube using visible video metadata, transcripts, and the user's own API key.\n\nfalse\n2025-08-26T19:20:17Z\n56548b55d75dfd868310994f001c2650f541ee3f\n2025-08-26T19:20:14Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/VidQuery\nproject-vidquery\nassets/projects/vidquery.png\nhttps://vidquery.netlify.app\n\nhttps://github.com/montasim/VidQuery/releases/latest\nWXT\nTypeScript\nReact 19\nChrome Manifest V3\nChrome Extensions API\nTailwind CSS\nGemini API\nZod\nTanStack Start\nVitest\nReact Testing","YouTube content extraction.\nPage integration\nReact extension UI and request state.\nExtension interface\nValidated transcript/context envelope and Gemini adapter.\nProvider boundary\nBrowser storage/permissions plus release-built Manifest V3 package. The companion web app documents and distributes the product.\nStorage and delivery\nYouTube content extraction; React extension UI and request state; validated transcript/context envelope and Gemini adapter; browser storage/permissions plus release-built Manifest V3"
  - id: "project:project-thoughtline:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Thoughtline - LinkedIn Writing Assistant project?
    text: "Montasim built Thoughtline - LinkedIn Writing Assistant, an extension project. User-controlled Chrome side-panel extension for drafting replies from pasted or selected LinkedIn posts, refining writing, and keeping every publish action manual. His role covered product design and browser-extension engineering, with scope spanning browser integration, provider workflows, and privacy. Users can paste a post or select a visible conversation and draft four reply directions. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-thoughtline","case-study:thoughtline:problem","case-study:thoughtline:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "User-controlled Chrome side-panel extension for drafting replies from pasted or selected LinkedIn posts, refining writing, and keeping every publish action manual.\n\nfalse\n2026-07-17T21:02:58Z\nb2085eead2f039535d2caf81c901d57c0028298a\n2026-07-17T20:56:56Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Thoughtline\nproject-thoughtline\nassets/projects/thoughtline.png\nhttps://thoughtline.netlify.app\n\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Thoughtline/releases/latest\nWXT\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nChrome Manifest V3\nChrome Extensions API\nTailwind","Writing a useful LinkedIn reply or post requires understanding the visible conversation while preserving the author's own voice, intent, and control.\nLinkedIn page content is untrusted and must be bounded before entering prompts.\nOpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq keys are all required so the fixed free-only route can fall back without asking during a writing task.\nExtension permissions, encrypted local storage, and retention/export behavior must remain understandable to the user.","Users can paste a post or select a visible conversation and draft four reply directions.\nGenerate/refine post ideas in configured voice.\nUse a fixed free-only OpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq route under defined fallback rules.\nExport and restore validated configurations and history."
