  - id: "case-study:postcraft:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the PostCraft case study?
    text: "Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools. Montasim owned product design and full-stack engineering, covering AI workflow, publishing integrations, and background jobs. The solution also had to respect “AI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas”; “LinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the PostCraft solution?
    text: "The Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules, infrastructure in core services, and durable delivery in Inngest. A representative decision was “Separate provider choice from generation”: A provider switcher walks configured models and keys, so product workflows do not depend on one vendor-specific client. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews\nWorkspace\nValidated workflows for content, library, accounts, and publishing\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI with model and key fallback\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persistence, Inngest jobs, and social platform APIs\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules, infrastructure in core services, and durable delivery in Inngest.\nA provider switcher walks configured models and"
  - id: "case-study:postcraft:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with PostCraft?
    text: "Montasim defined the end-to-end product flow from topic input to platform-ready output. He built the Next.js interface, API routes, domain services, and MongoDB repositories. He implemented multi-provider AI selection, reusable guardrails, scoring, and platform previews. He integrated authentication, account recovery, social OAuth, Inngest scheduling, and publishing records. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery”; “Provider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint”; “Publishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation”; “Operational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:postcraft:contribution","case-study:postcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Defined the end-to-end product flow from topic input to platform-ready output.\nBuilt the Next.js interface, API routes, domain services, and MongoDB repositories.\nImplemented multi-provider AI selection, reusable guardrails, scoring, and platform previews.\nIntegrated authentication, account recovery, social OAuth, Inngest scheduling, and publishing records.","One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery.\nProvider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint.\nPublishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation.\nOperational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access."
  - id: "case-study:b4joinacompany:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the b4joinacompany case study?
    text: "Job seekers in Bangladesh often assemble company research from workplace stories, salary submissions, official pages, and interview conversations. Those sources have different levels of reliability, but conventional summaries can flatten them into an unfair good-or-bad judgment. Montasim owned product design, evidence modeling, and full-stack engineering, covering decision support, cited AI, private research, and versioned data. The solution also had to respect “Personal workplace reports provide context, not verified statements of current company policy”; “Company names and salary records can be ambiguous, so fuzzy matching cannot silently publish a result”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
