    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “HTTP Status Codes Are Data, Not a Switch Statement”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “HTTP Status Codes Are Data, Not a Switch Statement” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Developers need status-code names, categories, and lifecycle metadata without memorized numeric checks, stale hand-written lists, or a heavy dependency. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:http-status-codes-are-data","case-study:http-status-lite:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Library design\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-http-status-lite\nhttp-status-codes-are-data\nHTTP Status Codes Are Data, Not a Switch Statement\ntools","Developers need status-code names, categories, and lifecycle metadata without memorized numeric checks, stale hand-written lists, or a heavy dependency.\nIANA registry state changes over time.\nObsolete, temporary, and unused codes must remain distinguishable.\nAPI typing and ESM/CommonJS compatibility must not depend on live network access."
  - id: "blog:mime-lookup-is-not-content-validation"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Filename-to-MIME Lookup Is Useful—Until You Mistake It for Validation”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Filename-to-MIME Lookup Is Useful—Until You Mistake It for Validation” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Upload, download, and HTTP code needs dependable mappings between filenames/extensions and media types without carrying a broad database dependency into every bundle. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:mime-lookup-is-not-content-validation","case-study:mime-types-lite:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Library design\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-mime-types-lite\nmime-lookup-is-not-content-validation\nFilename-to-MIME Lookup Is Useful—Until You Mistake It for Validation\ntools","Upload, download, and HTTP code needs dependable mappings between filenames/extensions and media types without carrying a broad database dependency into every bundle.\nOne extension can have aliases or ambiguous mappings.\nLookup must normalize case/dots/paths without pretending file content was inspected.\nESM/CommonJS consumers require stable typed exports with no runtime dependencies."
  - id: "blog:designing-an-api-that-admits-user-agent-uncertainty"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “User-Agent Parsing Is Heuristic. I Designed the API to Admit It”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “User-Agent Parsing Is Heuristic. I Designed the API to Admit It” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Analytics and diagnostics need a normalized view of client strings and hints, while many parsers are heavy or encourage unsafe use of inherently heuristic data. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:designing-an-api-that-admits-user-agent-uncertainty","case-study:client-parser:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Library design\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-client-parser\ndesigning-an-api-that-admits-user-agent-uncertainty\nUser-Agent Parsing Is Heuristic. I Designed the API to Admit It\ntools","Analytics and diagnostics need a normalized view of client strings and hints, while many parsers are heavy or encourage unsafe use of inherently heuristic data.\nUser-Agent classification is heuristic and spoofable.\nClient Hints availability varies and may be privacy-restricted.\nResults cannot safely drive authentication, authorization, or capability decisions."
  - id: "blog:company-research-without-a-fake-score"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “How I Modeled Company Research Without Producing a Fake Score”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “How I Modeled Company Research Without Producing a Fake Score” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Job seekers in Bangladesh often assemble company research from workplace stories, salary submissions, official pages, and interview conversations. Those sources have different levels of reliability, but conventional summaries can flatten them into an unfair good-or-bad judgment. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:company-research-without-a-fake-score","case-study:b4joinacompany:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Evidence systems\ntrue\ncase-study-derived\nproject-b4joinacompany\ncompany-research-without-a-fake-score\nHow I Modeled Company Research Without Producing a Fake Score\ncareer","Job seekers in Bangladesh often assemble company research from workplace stories, salary submissions, official pages, and interview conversations. Those sources have different levels of reliability, but conventional summaries can flatten them into an unfair good-or-bad judgment.\nPersonal workplace reports provide context, not verified statements of current company policy.\nCompany names and salary records can be ambiguous, so fuzzy matching cannot silently publish a result.\nAI answers must remain bounded by"
  - id: "blog:a-modular-monolith-for-a-book-platform"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Why I Chose a Modular Monolith for a Multi-Sided Book Platform”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Why I Chose a Modular Monolith for a Multi-Sided Book Platform” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Readers, communities, marketplace activity, library operations, and AI-assisted discovery are commonly split across separate systems with disconnected identity and catalog data. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:a-modular-monolith-for-a-book-platform","case-study:book-heaven:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "System design\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-book-heaven\na-modular-monolith-for-a-book-platform\nWhy I Chose a Modular Monolith for a Multi-Sided Book Platform\nproduct","Readers, communities, marketplace activity, library operations, and AI-assisted discovery are commonly split across separate systems with disconnected identity and catalog data.\nThe broad feature set has many service dependencies and is beta, not a turnkey production claim.\nReal-time messaging is optional and falls back to HTTP polling.\nAI, embeddings/pgvector, payments, email, Redis queues, Firebase, and Socket.IO each require separate configuration and operational review."
  - id: "blog:dynamic-readme-svgs-without-stored-images"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Generating Dynamic README SVGs Without Storing a Single Image”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Generating Dynamic README SVGs Without Storing a Single Image” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Creating a customized typing animation for a README normally requires manual SVG/CSS editing and hosting the result. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:dynamic-readme-svgs-without-stored-images","case-study:markdown-typing-svg:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "API design\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-markdown-typing-svg\ndynamic-readme-svgs-without-stored-images\nGenerating Dynamic README SVGs Without Storing a Single Image\ntools","Creating a customized typing animation for a README normally requires manual SVG/CSS editing and hosting the result.\nPublic embeds depend on deployment availability.\nSuccessful responses cache for 24 hours.\nThere is no documented request-rate/abuse-control layer and SVG text must be safely escaped/validated."
  - id: "blog:one-lifecycle-model-for-links-text-and-qr"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “URLs, Text Shares, and QR Codes Needed One Lifecycle Model”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “URLs, Text Shares, and QR Codes Needed One Lifecycle Model” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Sharing links, expiring text/code, and QR payloads often requires separate tools and gives users little control over slugs, access, expiry, or basic view/click records. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:one-lifecycle-model-for-links-text-and-qr","case-study:shrnkly:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "System design\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-shrnkly\none-lifecycle-model-for-links-text-and-qr\nURLs, Text Shares, and QR Codes Needed One Lifecycle Model\nproduct","Sharing links, expiring text/code, and QR payloads often requires separate tools and gives users little control over slugs, access, expiry, or basic view/click records.\nPassword protection is application access control, not safe secret storage.\nClick/view logging has privacy and retention implications.\nInstalled Redis and checked-in Docker artifacts are not documented as active/reliable runtime paths."
  - id: "blog:a-readme-counter-measures-requests-not-people"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A README Counter Measures Requests, Not People”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A README Counter Measures Requests, Not People” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: README authors need an image-embeddable counter endpoint because Markdown cannot run application JavaScript or call an API and render arbitrary data directly. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:a-readme-counter-measures-requests-not-people","case-study:github-readme-counter:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "API design\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-github-readme-counter\na-readme-counter-measures-requests-not-people\nA README Counter Measures Requests, Not People\ntools","README authors need an image-embeddable counter endpoint because Markdown cannot run application JavaScript or call an API and render arbitrary data directly.\nRequests from bots, caches, renderers, and refreshes are counted, so values are not unique-human analytics.\nFile persistence is single-process and can reset on ephemeral filesystems.\nThe endpoint has no authentication or documented rate limiting."
  - id: "blog:prayer-time-software-needs-provenance"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Prayer-Time Software Needs Provenance, Not False Precision”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Prayer-Time Software Needs Provenance, Not False Precision” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Sehri/Iftar schedules need a clear local-time public view and an auditable maintenance workflow because external prayer-time sources, district selection, and timetable corrections can change the result. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:prayer-time-software-needs-provenance","case-study:ramadan-clock:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Data engineering\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-ramadan-clock\nprayer-time-software-needs-provenance\nPrayer-Time Software Needs Provenance, Not False Precision\nproduct","Sehri/Iftar schedules need a clear local-time public view and an auditable maintenance workflow because external prayer-time sources, district selection, and timetable corrections can change the result.\nCalculated times depend on source, method, location, and local authority.\nExternal fetching requires retry/rate limiting and must not overwrite validated data indiscriminately.\nDuplicate date/location rows and malformed bulk imports must be rejected."
  - id: "blog:fifty-developer-tools-without-fifty-architectures"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “How I Scaled 50+ Developer Utilities Without 50 Architectures”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “How I Scaled 50+ Developer Utilities Without 50 Architectures” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Small development tasks often send people across unrelated single-purpose websites. Each tool has a different interface, unclear privacy behavior, and no shared path for search, history, saved work, or reuse. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:fifty-developer-tools-without-fifty-architectures","case-study:devtools:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Frontend architecture\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-devtools\nfifty-developer-tools-without-fifty-architectures\nHow I Scaled 50+ Developer Utilities Without 50 Architectures\ntools","Small development tasks often send people across unrelated single-purpose websites. Each tool has a different interface, unclear privacy behavior, and no shared path for search, history, saved work, or reuse.\nMany transformations should stay in the browser, while network diagnostics necessarily contact a target or server endpoint.\nA catalog with more than 50 workflows must remain quick to search, navigate, and extend without duplicating page infrastructure.\nSaved items, shares, webhooks, and short links introduce"
  - id: "blog:scheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Scheduled Publishing Cannot Depend on an Open Browser Tab”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Scheduled Publishing Cannot Depend on an Open Browser Tab” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:scheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab","case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Distributed systems\ntrue\ncase-study-derived\nproject-postcraft\nscheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab\nScheduled Publishing Cannot Depend on an Open Browser Tab\nproduct","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "blog:keeping-tax-id-lookups-out-of-the-server"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “I Kept Tax-ID Lookups Out of the Server”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “I Kept Tax-ID Lookups Out of the Server” is framed as “The visible feature was only the surface”: A static historical audit list is inconvenient to search manually, especially on mobile, while submitting a tax identifier to another application server creates avoidable query exposure. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:keeping-tax-id-lookups-out-of-the-server","case-study:tin-audit-checker:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Privacy engineering\nfalse\ncase-study-derived\nproject-tin-audit-checker\nkeeping-tax-id-lookups-out-of-the-server\nI Kept Tax-ID Lookups Out of the Server\nproduct","A static historical audit list is inconvenient to search manually, especially on mobile, while submitting a tax identifier to another application server creates avoidable query exposure.\nThe data is historical, not live audit/compliance status.\nClaimed NBR provenance, accuracy, completeness, and redistribution rights are not established.\nShipping the dataset to browsers makes records retrievable even though lookup queries stay client-side."
