  - id: "catalog-chronology-comparison:evidence-relationship"
    category: catalog-chronology-comparison
    question: "How do Montasim's projects, case studies, and blog articles relate?"
    text: "A project record identifies the product and stack; its case study explains problem, constraints, architecture, contribution, and outcomes; a project-linked blog article presents that same evidence as a readable engineering narrative. The layers are related views, not three independent accomplishments."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:architecture","blog:scheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "AI-powered social media manager to generate, preview, score, and auto-publish engaging posts for LinkedIn, X, and Facebook using Gemini AI and custom brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq API\nZhipuAI\nInngest\nREST APIs\nTailwind","Generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews\nWorkspace\nValidated workflows for content, library, accounts, and publishing\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI with model and key fallback\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persistence, Inngest jobs, and social platform APIs\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules, infrastructure in core services, and durable delivery in Inngest.\nA provider switcher walks configured models and","Distributed systems\ntrue\ncase-study-derived\nproject-postcraft\nscheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab\nScheduled Publishing Cannot Depend on an Open Browser Tab\nproduct"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:end-to-end-delivery"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What evidence shows Montasim can deliver a product end to end?
    text: "Montasim's case studies trace delivery from a defined problem through constraints, architecture, implementation, and outcomes across AI SaaS, extensions, data products, packages, and developer tools. PostCraft is a strong full-path example: he handled generation, brand controls, previews, scheduling, durable delivery, and provider integration. For a client, that is evidence he can reason about the whole product lifecycle rather than hand off responsibility at the UI boundary."
    factIds[4]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:problem","case-study:postcraft:contribution","case-study:postcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[4]: "AI-powered social media manager to generate, preview, score, and auto-publish engaging posts for LinkedIn, X, and Facebook using Gemini AI and custom brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq API\nZhipuAI\nInngest\nREST APIs\nTailwind","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status.","Defined the end-to-end product flow from topic input to platform-ready output.\nBuilt the Next.js interface, API routes, domain services, and MongoDB repositories.\nImplemented multi-provider AI selection, reusable guardrails, scoring, and platform previews.\nIntegrated authentication, account recovery, social OAuth, Inngest scheduling, and publishing records.","One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery.\nProvider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint.\nPublishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation.\nOperational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:client-needs"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: Has Montasim worked directly from client requirements?
    text: "Yes. As a Web Developer at Codez, Montasim analyzed client needs, implemented the appropriate functionality, and created and executed test scenarios. His later case studies show the same skill at greater depth by turning user and business constraints into explicit technical boundaries. A prospective client can ask him to apply that process to their users, risks, integrations, and definition of success."
    factIds[2]: "experience:experience-codez-web-developer","case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Codez Info Tech.\n\nDeveloped and maintained **web applications with PHP and JavaScript**. Analyzed **client needs** and developed appropriate functionality. Created and executed **test scenarios** to ensure quality deliverables.\nexperience-codez-web-developer\nNorth Badda, Dhaka, Bangladesh\nC\n/images/organizations/codez.png\nMay 2021 - Nov 2021\nWeb Developer\nPHP\nJavaScript\nHTML\nCSS","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:publishing-product"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What product judgment does PostCraft demonstrate?
    text: "PostCraft treats social publishing as a lifecycle rather than a prompt box: one idea moves through generation, brand controls, platform previews, review, scheduling, and durable delivery. The architecture separates interactive work from background execution and keeps delivery status reviewable."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:architecture","case-study:postcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "AI-powered social media manager to generate, preview, score, and auto-publish engaging posts for LinkedIn, X, and Facebook using Gemini AI and custom brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq API\nZhipuAI\nInngest\nREST APIs\nTailwind","Generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews\nWorkspace\nValidated workflows for content, library, accounts, and publishing\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI with model and key fallback\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persistence, Inngest jobs, and social platform APIs\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules, infrastructure in core services, and durable delivery in Inngest.\nA provider switcher walks configured models and","One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery.\nProvider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint.\nPublishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation.\nOperational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:privacy-product"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What client-side privacy decision did Montasim make for tax-ID lookup?
    text: "For TIN Audit Checker, Montasim kept the 72,342-record lookup in the browser so the queried identifier did not need to reach an application server. The case study scopes the product to a historical list and avoids implying that a match is a current legal determination."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-tin-audit-checker","case-study:tin-audit-checker:architecture","case-study:tin-audit-checker:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Check if your Bangladesh TIN is selected for NBR Risk-Based Audit (AY 2023-24). Client-side, private, instant lookup of 72,342 returns.\n\nfalse\n2026-04-30T09:35:36Z\n7d915fdb881c99faea75b5ce829d15dce17359ba\n2026-04-30T11:59:39Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/tin-audit-checker\nproject-tin-audit-checker\nassets/projects/tin-audit-checker.png\nhttps://tin-audit-checker.netlify.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nJSON\nSheetJS\nTIN Audit Checker -","Next.js lookup UI.\nSource records\nSession-cached static JSON dataset.\nTransformation\nClient-side normalization and exact matching with no TIN submission API.\nRelease formats\nNext.js lookup UI; session-cached static JSON dataset; client-side normalization and exact matching with no TIN submission API.\nPerform lookup entirely in the browser to avoid server receipt of entered TINs.\nPerform lookup entirely in the browser\nNormalize formatting characters but require an exact digit match.\nNormalize formatting characters","Users can search the historical list quickly.\nLookup text is not submitted to an application API.\nMatches return bounded mapped details.\nNon-matches and matches are both framed as requiring current official confirmation."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:job-seeker-product"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How did Montasim design company research without unfair scoring?
    text: "b4joinacompany keeps workplace stories, salary submissions, official sources, and interview evidence labeled by provenance instead of flattening them into a good-or-bad company score. That gives job seekers useful context while making uncertainty and source quality visible."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-b4joinacompany","case-study:b4joinacompany:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Research a company before you apply, interview, or accept an offer.\n\ntrue\n2026-07-26T04:31:13Z\n9849284bf1ac0b399c7b9d9f59bbbb9b36ba9629\n2026-07-26T04:30:07Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/b4joinacompany\nproject-b4joinacompany\nassets/projects/b4joinacompany.png\nhttps://b4joinacompany.netlify.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nMongoDB\nGemini API\nGroq API\nBetter Auth\nREST APIs\nZod\nVitest\nSystem Design\nb4joinacompany\ncareer-tools\ncompany-research\ndue-diligence\nemployment\njob-search\nweb-application\nwebsite","Versioned company, story, comment, and salary evidence records\nSource releases\nDeterministic identity matching, evidence labels, and question ranking\nResearch layer\nNext.js research briefs, compare, Ask, and a versioned REST API\nProduct surfaces\nBetter Auth and MongoDB checkpoints pinned to evidence revisions\nPrivate workspace\nVersioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while private checkpoints live separately in MongoDB.\nReported,"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:configurable-platform"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How did Montasim avoid separate codebases for different education sites?
    text: "EduCanvas separates institution theme and content configuration from shared publishing, identity, and delivery workflows. The result supports multiple institution types without maintaining a forked application for each client, reducing drift while preserving controlled variation."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-educanvas","case-study:educanvas:architecture","case-study:educanvas:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Theme-driven educational website and content-management starter built with Next.js\n\nfalse\n2026-07-27T17:41:53Z\nf0ea922b85f28025d1e3bf81d5a9b4ff413cda56\n2026-07-27T17:50:31Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/EduCanvas\nproject-educanvas\nassets/projects/educanvas.png\n\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nMongoDB\nBetter Auth\nGoogle Drive API\nResend\nREST APIs\nRadix UI\nPlaywright\nSystem Design\nEduCanvas\ncontent-management\neducation\nnextjs\ntailwindcss\ntypescript\nwebsite-starter\ntemplate","Next.js public/admin routes.\nApplication shell\nFeature-owned content/admission/media domain modules.\nContent services\nCentralized institution and visual-theme configuration/tokens.\nIntegrations\nMongoDB/auth/Drive/email adapters with server-boundary enforcement.\nQuality\nNext.js public/admin routes; feature-owned content/admission/media domain modules; centralized institution and visual-theme configuration/tokens; MongoDB/auth/Drive/email adapters with server-boundary enforcement.\nSplit institution identity tokens","Developers can deploy one starter for three institution categories.\nAdministrators can publish content and manage enquiries.\nVisitors get configured branding/layout on shared routes.\nDeployments can fall back to read-only repository defaults where documented."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:release-confidence"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How could Montasim improve delivery confidence for a client project?
    text: "His release-oriented agent skills validate builds, packages, tags, CI, install instructions, metadata, deployments, and public documentation as separate verifiable states. That approach makes partial failure visible and reduces the risk of declaring a release complete based on one system alone."
    factIds[3]: "case-study:ship-agent-skill:architecture","case-study:verify-project-release:outcomes","case-study:prepare-netlify-deployment:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Agent-facing orchestration in SKILL.md.\nSkill workflow\nDeterministic preflight/release-gate references.\nAutomation\nNpm installer/package contract and consumer-side tests.\nVerification\nAgent-facing orchestration in SKILL.md; deterministic preflight/release-gate references; npm installer/package contract and consumer-side tests.\nModel release stages separately rather than treating \"ship\" as blanket permission.\nModel release stages separately\nValidate host portability before creating immutable artifacts.\nValidate","Agents can detect version/tag drift.\nDistinguish missing artifacts from blocked verification.\nReport exact failing surfaces.\nProvide evidence links without claiming an install was executed.","Project/framework/lockfile detection.\nSkill workflow\nDeterministic netlify.toml and output audit using documented patterns.\nAutomation\nLocal build/route verification and authorization-gated remote handoff.\nVerification\nProject/framework/lockfile detection; deterministic netlify.toml and output audit using documented patterns; local build/route verification and authorization-gated remote handoff.\nDerive an explicit build contract before changing configuration.\nDerive an explicit build contract before changing"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:bounded-delivery"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: Can Montasim deliver a focused tool as well as a large platform?
    text: "Yes. His catalog ranges from narrow typed packages and single-purpose audit or SVG tools to multi-sided SaaS and healthcare platforms. The smaller case studies show deliberate scope control, while larger systems show authentication, persistence, scheduling, real-time behavior, and operational workflows."
    factIds[4]: "project:project-content-types-lite","project:project-tin-audit-checker","project:project-postcraft","experience:experience-mymedicalhub-senior-software-engineer"
    supportingExcerpts[4]: "Tiny, type-safe HTTP media type constants and utilities for parsing, formatting, matching, and validation.\n\nfalse\n2025-01-29T13:00:26Z\n78eeb6ca02149b23e1486078e8b02ac009faf0b5\n2025-01-29T11:05:22Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/content-types-lite\nproject-content-types-lite\nassets/projects/content-types-lite.png\nhttps://content-types-lite.netlify.app\nhttps://www.npmjs.com/package/content-types-lite\nTypeScript\nESM\nCommonJS\ntsup\nTanStack Start\nReact\nTailwind CSS\nVite\nVitest\nNetlify\nGitHub","Check if your Bangladesh TIN is selected for NBR Risk-Based Audit (AY 2023-24). Client-side, private, instant lookup of 72,342 returns.\n\nfalse\n2026-04-30T09:35:36Z\n7d915fdb881c99faea75b5ce829d15dce17359ba\n2026-04-30T11:59:39Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/tin-audit-checker\nproject-tin-audit-checker\nassets/projects/tin-audit-checker.png\nhttps://tin-audit-checker.netlify.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nJSON\nSheetJS\nTIN Audit Checker -","AI-powered social media manager to generate, preview, score, and auto-publish engaging posts for LinkedIn, X, and Facebook using Gemini AI and custom brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq API\nZhipuAI\nInngest\nREST APIs\nTailwind","MyMedicalHub International Ltd.\nhttps://mymedicalhub.com\nArchitected an **FSM-based biometric engine** replacing unstable React hooks with deterministic state transitions for **99.9% reliability** during AI analysis. Optimized **AI pipelines** to support **60 FPS** pose estimation and real-time rep counting via adaptive frame rate logic. Led a full rewrite of the **medical assessment chatbot** with Redux Toolkit and refactored **54+ modules** into Presentational/Container"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:constraint-discovery"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How does Montasim uncover requirements beyond the visible feature?
    text: "His case studies explicitly separate the visible request from constraints such as trust, lifecycle, failure recovery, source provenance, accessibility, privacy, and ownership. This makes hidden product risks discussable before they become incidental implementation behavior."
    factIds[3]: "case-study:thoughtline:problem","case-study:postcraft:problem","case-study:foliofarer:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Writing a useful LinkedIn reply or post requires understanding the visible conversation while preserving the author's own voice, intent, and control.\nLinkedIn page content is untrusted and must be bounded before entering prompts.\nOpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq keys are all required so the fixed free-only route can fall back without asking during a writing task.\nExtension permissions, encrypted local storage, and retention/export behavior must remain understandable to the user.","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status.","A memorable 3D portfolio can hide the information recruiters need and exclude visitors whose devices, preferences, or assistive technology do not suit WebGL exploration.\nGenerated worlds must be deterministic enough for navigation and tests.\nContent must remain fully reachable without relying on 3D interaction.\nRendering must adapt to reduced motion/data, viewport, connection, and device capability."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:business-economics"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What evidence shows Montasim considers project economics?
    text: "The clearest professional result is a 70% infrastructure-cost reduction from migrating Azure VMs to App Service and automating CI/CD. Product architectures such as shared EduCanvas workflows and common DevTools contracts also address duplication and maintenance cost, though those records do not publish monetary figures."
    factIds[3]: "experience:experience-mymedicalhub-software-engineer","case-study:educanvas:architecture","case-study:devtools:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "MyMedicalHub International Ltd.\nhttps://mymedicalhub.com\nBoosted application performance by **40%** by stripping legacy UI libraries and optimizing React rendering cycles. Achieved **70% infrastructure cost reduction** by migrating Azure VMs to App Service and automating CI/CD pipelines. Deployed a **HIPAA-compliant SSO system** with multi-role access, OAuth integration, and strict CSP headers.\nexperience-mymedicalhub-software-engineer\nMohakhali, Dhaka, Bangladesh\nM\n/images/organizations/mymedicalhub.png\nOct 2023","Next.js public/admin routes.\nApplication shell\nFeature-owned content/admission/media domain modules.\nContent services\nCentralized institution and visual-theme configuration/tokens.\nIntegrations\nMongoDB/auth/Drive/email adapters with server-boundary enforcement.\nQuality\nNext.js public/admin routes; feature-owned content/admission/media domain modules; centralized institution and visual-theme configuration/tokens; MongoDB/auth/Drive/email adapters with server-boundary enforcement.\nSplit institution identity tokens","Searchable navigation, routes, labels, and keyboard discovery\nTool registry\nFocused formatters, generators, testers, inspectors, and references\nFeature modules\nLocal transforms, preferences, input history, and reusable state\nBrowser boundary\nNext.js APIs, Better Auth, PostgreSQL, Prisma, and target services\nServer boundary\nA navigation registry acts as the catalog source of truth. Tool features own their tabs and logic, shared app infrastructure handles auth and storage, and route handlers isolate network and"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:data-trust"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How does Montasim make data-heavy products more trustworthy?
    text: "The Bangladesh Location Registry retains source provenance through normalization, while company research preserves evidence labels and the TIN checker keeps lookups private and historically scoped. Across these products, the design avoids presenting normalized data as more certain or current than its sources allow."
    factIds[3]: "case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:architecture","case-study:b4joinacompany:architecture","case-study:tin-audit-checker:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Source/provenance manifests.\nSource records\nNormalized records governed by JSON Schema.\nTransformation\nJavaScript validation/build scripts.\nRelease formats\nVersioned JSON, JSONL, compressed, and SQLite release outputs with samples/docs.\nValidation\nSource/provenance manifests; normalized records governed by JSON Schema; JavaScript validation/build scripts; versioned JSON, JSONL, compressed, and SQLite release outputs with samples/docs.\nPreserve source lineage and limitations beside normalized data instead of hiding","Versioned company, story, comment, and salary evidence records\nSource releases\nDeterministic identity matching, evidence labels, and question ranking\nResearch layer\nNext.js research briefs, compare, Ask, and a versioned REST API\nProduct surfaces\nBetter Auth and MongoDB checkpoints pinned to evidence revisions\nPrivate workspace\nVersioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while private checkpoints live separately in MongoDB.\nReported,","Next.js lookup UI.\nSource records\nSession-cached static JSON dataset.\nTransformation\nClient-side normalization and exact matching with no TIN submission API.\nRelease formats\nNext.js lookup UI; session-cached static JSON dataset; client-side normalization and exact matching with no TIN submission API.\nPerform lookup entirely in the browser to avoid server receipt of entered TINs.\nPerform lookup entirely in the browser\nNormalize formatting characters but require an exact digit match.\nNormalize formatting characters"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:multi-platform-product"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What does Montasim understand about multi-platform publishing?
    text: "PostCraft models one idea through platform-specific generation, scoring, previews, brand constraints, scheduling, and delivery to LinkedIn, X, and Facebook. The workflow acknowledges that platforms have different limits and that durable publishing cannot depend on a browser tab remaining open."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:problem","case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "AI-powered social media manager to generate, preview, score, and auto-publish engaging posts for LinkedIn, X, and Facebook using Gemini AI and custom brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq API\nZhipuAI\nInngest\nREST APIs\nTailwind","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status.","Generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews\nWorkspace\nValidated workflows for content, library, accounts, and publishing\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI with model and key fallback\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persistence, Inngest jobs, and social platform APIs\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules, infrastructure in core services, and durable delivery in Inngest.\nA provider switcher walks configured models and"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:working-style"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What working style could a client expect from Montasim?
    text: "Clients can expect a proactive, clear, collaborative, and detail-conscious working style, based on attributed feedback from managers and teammates. His case studies reinforce the same pattern: he makes scope, constraints, technical decisions, outcomes, and limitations explicit. In practice, that supports transparent trade-off conversations and reduces the risk of discovering important assumptions only after implementation."
    factIds[3]: "recommendation:1:tabbi-quadir","recommendation:3:shahriar-iqbal","case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "May 25, 2025\nTabbi Quadir\nmanaged Mohammad Montasim Al Mamun directly\nProud contributor in accelerating digital transformation in Bangladesh\nI highly recommend Montasim Al Mamun as a skilled and dedicated software engineer. I had the pleasure of working with Montasim at MMHI.\n\nDuring our time together, I was consistently impressed by his technical abilities and commitment to delivering high-quality work. He demonstrated strong problem-solving skills and took a proactive approach to addressing challenges.\n\nMontasim","July 1, 2024\nShahriar Iqbal\nworked with Mohammad Montasim Al Mamun but on different teams\nSoftware QA Engineer | Postman | JMeter | Agile | JIRA | Azure DevOps | Researcher | Explorer\nI have worked closely with Mohammad Montasim -Al- Mamun Shuvo at MyMedicalHUB International Ltd., where he has consistently demonstrated his exceptional expertise in frontend web development. Mohammad is an outstanding professional in every sense, and I wholeheartedly recommend him.\n\nMohammad possesses a deep knowledge of React,","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:scope-conversation"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What should a potential client discuss with Montasim before starting work?
    text: "Discuss the target users, business outcome, privacy and security constraints, reliability expectations, integrations, delivery environment, ownership after launch, and the evidence that will prove success. Then align price, current capacity, timeline, milestones, communication cadence, and engagement terms, which are not published in the portfolio. This gives Montasim enough context to recommend a bounded first phase instead of estimating a product whose scope is still unclear."
    factIds[2]: profile,"case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Most software looks great in the demo. I build the kind that survives production.\n\nI'm a **Senior Software Engineer** with **3+ years** building real-time, AI-driven, multi-tenant SaaS platforms across the full stack: React, Next.js, Node.js, and TypeScript. Promoted twice at the same company, I now own frontend architecture across a multi-service platform, lead PR reviews, and mentor the engineers behind me.\n\nKey highlights: cut cloud infrastructure costs **70%** by migrating Azure VMs to App Service with","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:proof-of-work"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What proof can a client review before contacting Montasim?
    text: "Before making contact, a client can review 32 project records, 32 structured case studies, repository links and verified commits, live or package links where published, technical articles, professional experience, credentials, and attributed recommendations. The most useful approach is to select the closest comparable case and ask Montasim what would transfer, what would change, and which risks he would validate first for the new project."
    factIds[3]: "derived:catalog-count:projects","derived:catalog-count:casestudy","derived:catalog-count:recommendations"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "projects\n32","casestudy\n32","recommendations\n16"
